\lispPackageChapter{candlesticks/bars}

This is the package that reads and writes the candlesticks themselves.
A candlestick is the price of a numerator instrument denominated in a
denominator instrument, over a given duration, together with the retrieval
that produced it.  For example, a \texttt{BTC/USD} daily bar has Bitcoin
as its numerator, the US Dollar as its denominator, and the day duration.

The unique key includes the retrieval, so the same numbers from two
sources --- or from two retrievals of the same source --- are two rows.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{candlestick}

A CLOS class with slots \texttt{id}, \lispId{numerator-id},
\lispId{denominator-id}, \lispId{duration-id}, \lispId{retrieval-id},
\texttt{time}, \texttt{open}, \texttt{high}, \texttt{low}, \texttt{close},
\texttt{volume}, and \lispId{adjusted-close}.  The accessors have the
obvious names.  \texttt{time} is a universal time.  \texttt{volume} and
\texttt{adjusted-close} may be \texttt{nil}.  \texttt{open}/\texttt{high}/
\texttt{low}/\texttt{close} are as-traded; \texttt{adjusted-close} is the
source's split-adjusted close when it reported one.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{instrument-id-for}

Return the database \textsc{Uuid} of \lispVar{instrument}.
\lispVar{instrument} may be an \texttt{instrument} object, or a ticker
string resolved (and created if needed) at \texttt{:source}.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(instrument-id-for \lispVar{instrument} \&key \lispVar{name} \lispVar{instrument-type} \lispVar{source} \lispVar{exchange} \lispVar{at})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{duration-id-for}

Return the database \textsc{Uuid} of \lispVar{duration}.
\lispVar{duration} may be a \texttt{duration} object, a short name
(\texttt{"d"}, \texttt{"w"}, \ldots), a long name (\texttt{"day"},
\texttt{"week"}, \ldots), or an existing duration \textsc{Uuid}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{insert-candlestick}

Insert a single candlestick given its already-resolved identifiers and
return the new row.  \lispVar{time} is a universal time; the price fields
are numbers; \lispVar{volume} may be \texttt{nil}.  A conflicting
$(\textit{numerator}$, $\textit{denominator}$, $\textit{duration}$,
$\textit{time}$, $\textit{retrieval})$ pair is a no-op.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(insert-candlestick \lispVar{numerator-id} \lispVar{denominator-id} \lispVar{duration-id} \lispVar{retrieval-id} \lispVar{time} \lispVar{open} \lispVar{high} \lispVar{low} \lispVar{close} \&optional \lispVar{volume} \lispVar{adjusted-close})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{insert-candlesticks}

Insert a batch of candlestick rows, each a
\texttt{(time open high low close volume)} or
\texttt{(time open high low close volume adjusted-close)} tuple, and return two values:
the number of rows inserted, and the retrieval that records the batch's
provenance (or \texttt{nil} when \texttt{:retrieval-id} was supplied).

\lispVar{numerator} and \lispVar{denominator} are instrument symbols or
\texttt{instrument} objects; \lispVar{duration} is a duration (see
\texttt{duration-id-for}); \texttt{:source-name} names the data source.
When \texttt{:retrieval-id} is supplied, the bars are attached to that
existing retrieval instead of creating a new one.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(insert-candlesticks \lispVar{rows} \&key \lispVar{numerator} \lispVar{denominator} \lispVar{duration} \lispVar{retrieval-id} \lispVar{source-name} \lispVar{source-kind} \lispVar{endpoint} \lispVar{notes} \lispVar{retrieved-at} \lispVar{closed})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{ingest-candlesticks}

The high-level entry point for feeding data in from a source such as
CoinGecko or Yahoo Finance.  Store a batch of \textsc{Ohlc} candlesticks
in a single transaction and return two values: the number of rows
inserted, and the retrieval that records where they came from.

Each call records a new retrieval, so ingesting the same numbers again
stores a second instance of the data.  Inserting the same bars twice
against one existing retrieval is a no-op.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(ingest-candlesticks \lispVar{numerator} \lispVar{denominator} \lispVar{duration} \lispVar{bars} \&key \lispVar{source-name} \lispVar{source-kind} \lispVar{exchange} \lispVar{endpoint} \lispVar{notes} \lispVar{numerator-name} \lispVar{numerator-type} \lispVar{denominator-name} \lispVar{denominator-type} \lispVar{retrieved-at} \lispVar{at} \lispVar{closed})}}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{numerator}, \lispVar{denominator}] Instrument symbols or \texttt{instrument} objects.
\item[\lispVar{duration}] A short name, long name, \texttt{duration}, or id.
\item[\lispVar{bars}] A list of \texttt{(time open high low close volume)} or \texttt{(time open high low close volume adjusted-close)} tuples.  \lispVar{time} is a universal time; \lispVar{volume} and \lispVar{adjusted-close} may be \texttt{nil}.
\item[\lispVar{source-name}] The name of the data source, created if needed.
\item[\lispVar{closed}] \texttt{t} (the default), \texttt{nil}, or a function of each bar's universal time.  Sets \texttt{is\_closed}.  \texttt{t} stores the batch as finished history.
\end{description}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(ingest-candlesticks "BTC" "USD" "d"
  (list (list (encode-universal-time 0 0 0 18 8 2026 0)
              25000.0 26000.0 24000.0 25500.0 100.0))
  :source-name "CoinGecko" :source-kind "api"
  :numerator-name "Bitcoin" :numerator-type "cryptocurrency"
  :denominator-name "US Dollar" :denominator-type "currency")
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{get-candlesticks}

Return the candlesticks for \lispVar{numerator}/\lispVar{denominator} at
the given duration, in time-ascending order.  \texttt{:from} and
\texttt{:to}, when given, are universal times bounding the bar time
(inclusive).  \texttt{:source}, when given, restricts the result to bars
retrieved from that data source.

This function never inserts rows.  If the pair, duration, or source does
not already exist, the result is the empty list.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(get-candlesticks \lispVar{numerator} \lispVar{denominator} \lispVar{duration} \&key \lispVar{from} \lispVar{to} \lispVar{source} \lispVar{exchange} \lispVar{at})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{count-candlesticks}

The number of stored candlesticks for the pair at the given duration,
summed over all retrievals.  Returns 0 when the pair or duration is not
already present; this function never inserts rows.

\section{Projections}

These take a list of candlesticks and return a list of one field, in the
same order.  \texttt{candlestick-close-prices} is the series most often
handed to technical indicators.  \texttt{close-series} returns
\texttt{(time close)} pairs, a convenient shape for feeding straight into
a time series.

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{candlestick-times}
\item \texttt{candlestick-open-prices}
\item \texttt{candlestick-high-prices}
\item \texttt{candlestick-low-prices}
\item \texttt{candlestick-close-prices}
\item \texttt{candlestick-volumes}
\item \texttt{candlestick-adjusted-close-prices}
\item \texttt{close-series} --- as-traded \texttt{(time close)} pairs
\item \texttt{adjusted-close-series} --- split-adjusted \texttt{(time adjusted-close)} pairs
\end{itemize}
